  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "The name \"Oroville\" blends Spanish oro (gold) with French -ville, literally meaning \"Gold City,\" echoing its Gold Rush roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/south-oroville-186091"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "South Oroville sits beside Lake Oroville State Recreation Area, offering boating and access to the historic Edward Hyatt Power Plant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/south-oroville-186091"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early growth in the area was driven by hydraulic mining and the arrival of the Western Pacific Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/south-oroville-186091"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, South Oroville functions mainly as a residential suburb of the larger Oroville community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/south-oroville-186091"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "There is no clear boundary between South Oroville and the official city limits of Oroville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Oroville%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "South Oroville first appeared as an unincorporated community in the 1960 U.S. Census.",
